<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gympie</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gympie is a town in south-east Queensland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, on the Mary River. James Nash found gold there in 1867, and the rush that followed is credited with rescuing the young colony of Queensland from bankruptcy. Gold mining continued into the 1920s, and the town later became a centre for dairy farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gympie.jpg" alt="Gympie" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thirumurugan Ponnusamy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gympie_City_Town_Hall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gympie lies on the Mary River in the Greater Sunshine Coast, about 170.7 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a>. Its name derives from the Gubbi Gubbi word gimpi-gimpi, meaning stinging tree, which refers to Dendrocnide moroides, a tree with large round leaves that have properties similar to stinging nettles. Gubbi Gubbi is the Aboriginal language formerly spoken by the peoples of the Sunshine Coast and Gympie regions.</p><p>The river floods the city from time to time. Floods are recorded in 1870, 1873, 1893, 1955, 1968, 1974, 1989, 1992, 1999, 2011, 2013 and 2022, most of them between December and April after heavy rain in the headwaters to the south. The highest peak was on 2 February 1893, at 25.45 m. On 27 February 2022 the river reached 22.96 m, more than a metre above the 1999 peak of 21.9 m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Graziers were the first European settlers. On 16 October 1867 James Nash reported the discovery of payable alluvial gold, at a time when Queensland was suffering a severe economic depression. The settlement was first called Nashville, and the name was changed to Gympie in 1868 after the local tree. The Gympie Creek Post Office opened on 1 December 1867 and took the new name in 1868.</p><p>The goldfield filled quickly with institutions. In 1868 a slab hut called the Miner's Bethel served the Anglican, Catholic and Methodist congregations until each had built its own church, and a Cobb &amp; Co coach service between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> and Gympie began, running twice a week. The first school, One Mile Boys State School, opened on 20 September 1869. The railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portlaoise">Maryborough</a> was completed in 1881, and the North Coast railway linked Gympie to Brisbane in 1891. The state declared Gympie a town in 1903.</p><p>In 1882 a handful of macadamia seeds taken from trees at Gympie went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. Researchers who compared the genetic profiles of Queensland and Hawaiian trees in 2019 concluded that essentially all the Hawaiian trees descend from a small population of Gympie trees, possibly a single tree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gympie had an urban population of 22,424 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is the administrative centre of the Gympie Region. Gold mining, dairy farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and tourism have all figured in its economy. Many buildings of the gold-rush era are on the Queensland Heritage Register, among them the Town Hall, built in 1890, the Court House, the Old Gympie Post Office and the Railway Hotel of 1915. The Gympie Gold Rush Festival marks Nash's discovery each October, and the Valley Rattler tourist train has run along the Mary Valley since 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gympie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
